@node Automatic context injection
@section Automatic context injection

@findex gptel-extras-add-context-files
The function @code{gptel-extras-add-context-files} is added as @samp{:before} advice on @code{gptel-send}. Before every request@comma{} it checks the user options @code{gptel-extras-add-repo-map-to-context} and @code{gptel-extras-add-conventions-to-context} (@ref{Aider context options, , Aider context options}) and@comma{} when enabled@comma{} calls the corresponding commands to inject context.

@node Post-response hooks
@section Post-response hooks

@findex gptel-extras-alert-when-finished
The function @code{gptel-extras-alert-when-finished} is added to @code{gptel-post-response-functions}. After @samp{gptel} inserts a response@comma{} it checks @code{gptel-extras-alert-when-finished} (@ref{Alert options, , Alert options}) and@comma{} if the condition is met@comma{} displays an alert via the @samp{alert} package.

@findex gptel-extras-generate-latex-previews
The function @code{gptel-extras-generate-latex-previews} is intended for @code{gptel-post-response-functions}. When the current buffer is in Org mode and its @code{default-directory} matches @code{gptel-extras-dir}@comma{} the function generates @LaTeX{} previews by calling @code{org-latex-preview}. This provides automatic rendering of mathematical notation in @samp{gptel} conversation files.

@node Model-specific workarounds
@section Model-specific workarounds

@findex gptel-extras-set-mullvad
The function @code{gptel-extras-set-mullvad} is added as @samp{:around} advice on @code{gptel-curl-get-response}. When @code{gptel-model} is @code{gemini-pro}@comma{} it activates a Mullvad VPN connection (via @code{mullvad-connect-to-website}) before the request is sent@comma{} with an automatic disconnect timer set to @code{gptel-extras-gemini-mullvad-disconnect-after} minutes (@ref{Gemini and VPN options, , Gemini and VPN options}). This bypasses location restrictions that Gemini imposes in certain regions.

The VPN connection is made silently (no user prompt). The @samp{mullvad} package must be installed and configured separately.

@menu
* Fast JSON logging::
@end menu

@node Fast JSON logging
@subsection Fast JSON logging

@findex gptel-extras--fast-log
The function @code{gptel-extras--fast-log} is added as @samp{:around} advice on @code{gptel--log}. The upstream @code{gptel--log} function calls @code{json-pretty-print}@comma{} which uses @code{replace-region-contents} internally.  That function runs the O(n²) @samp{compareseq} diff algorithm@comma{} which freezes Emacs on large JSON payloads (such as full LLM request/response bodies).

This advice replaces the pretty-printing step with a direct parse-then-insert approach: the JSON data is parsed with @code{json-parse-string} and re-inserted with @code{json-encode} (with @code{json-encoding-pretty-print} bound to @samp{t})@comma{} avoiding the diff entirely.

@node Running queries programmatically
@section Running queries programmatically

@findex gptel-extras-run-query
The function @code{gptel-extras-run-query} creates a new @samp{gptel} buffer from a query string@comma{} optionally setting a specific backend and model. It is used internally by @code{gptel-extras-search-and-ask-model} (@ref{Search integration, , Search integration}) and can be called from Lisp for any programmatic query dispatch.

The buffer name is a slugified version of the query with the appropriate file extension. If @samp{BACKEND} or @samp{MODEL} are nil@comma{} the global defaults are used.

Tool use (@code{gptel-use-tools}) is disabled for this request@comma{} since it is a programmatic call that should produce a plain-text response without invoking any tools.

@node Setting backend and model
@section Setting backend and model

@findex gptel-extras-set-backend-and-model
The function @code{gptel-extras-set-backend-and-model} sets @code{gptel-backend} and @code{gptel-model} buffer-locally in the current Org buffer. Pass the backend name as a string and the model as a symbol. If either argument is nil@comma{} the current global value is used.

This is a helper function (not a command) used by @code{gptel-extras-run-query} (@ref{Running queries programmatically, , Running queries programmatically}) and @code{gptel-extras-search-and-ask-model} (@ref{Search integration, , Search integration}).

@node Buffer validation
@section Buffer validation

@findex gptel-extras-ensure-gptel-mode
The function @code{gptel-extras-ensure-gptel-mode} signals an error if @code{gptel-mode} is not active in the current buffer. It is used as a guard by commands such as @code{gptel-extras-continue-in-new-buffer} (@ref{Continuing in a new buffer, , Continuing in a new buffer}) that require an active @samp{gptel} session.

@node Repository identification
@section Repository identification

@findex gptel-extras-get-repo
The function @code{gptel-extras-get-repo} determines the repository path for the current request. In Org mode@comma{} it checks the @samp{GPTEL_REPO} property at the document level; if not set@comma{} it prompts the user and stores the selection. In Markdown mode@comma{} it reads or writes a @samp{GPTEL_REPO} key in YAML front matter. In other modes@comma{} it uses @code{project-root} if a project is detected@comma{} falling back to a directory prompt.

@findex gptel-extras-get-repo-map
The function @code{gptel-extras-get-repo-map} returns the repository map for the current project@comma{} using the cached version if it is still valid (checked via @code{gptel-extras-repo-map-cache-valid-p}). When the cache is stale or empty@comma{} it calls @samp{aider --show-repo-map} to generate a fresh map and stores it along with the current timestamp and git HEAD reference.

@node Preset macro
@section Preset macro

@findex gptel-extras-make-tool-presets
The macro @code{gptel-extras-make-tool-presets} creates two @samp{gptel} presets for a given set of tools: a base preset that sets the active tools to exactly the specified list@comma{} and an additive preset (with @samp{+} appended to the name) that appends the tools to whatever tools are already active. This is useful for defining reusable tool configurations.

For example:

@lisp
(gptel-extras-make-tool-presets tools-web-search
  "web search tools"
  '(web_search browse_url))
@end lisp

This creates presets named @samp{tools-web-search} (sets tools to the two specified) and @samp{tools-web-search+} (adds them to existing tools).

@node Automatic Claude model discovery
@chapter Automatic Claude model discovery

@findex gptel-extras-model-discovery-mode
Enable @code{gptel-extras-model-discovery-mode} after creating a gptel backend named
@samp{Claude}. The mode queries Anthropic's Models API asynchronously when enabled
and every 24 hours while Emacs is running. It uses that backend's existing API
key and makes no inference requests. No credentials are stored in a cache or
passed on the command line.

@vindex claude-opus-latest
@vindex claude-sonnet-latest
@vindex claude-haiku-latest
The variables @code{claude-opus-latest}@comma{} @code{claude-sonnet-latest}@comma{} and
@code{claude-haiku-latest} hold concrete model symbols. Discovery reads every page
before updating them. Within each family it chooses the highest numeric release
version@comma{} breaking ties by snapshot date@comma{} creation time@comma{} and ID@. Preview IDs and
unrecognized naming formats are excluded. The variables start as @code{nil}; there is
no persistent cache or manually maintained default model ID@.

@vindex gptel-extras-model-bindings
Set @code{gptel-extras-model-bindings} to an alist of settings and the alias variables
they should follow. Its default is @code{nil} (no managed settings). For example@comma{} an entry whose car is @code{gptel-model} and whose
cdr is @code{claude-sonnet-latest} updates the global gptel default after discovery.
The setting is updated on every successful refresh; ordinary @code{setq} assignments
elsewhere copy the alias's value only once. Buffer-local model choices are
preserved. Consumers with a separate backend setting must select @samp{Claude}.

@vindex gptel-extras-model-backend-bindings
For a backend with its own model list@comma{} use
@code{gptel-extras-model-backend-bindings}. This alist defaults to @code{nil}. Each entry
maps an existing backend name to a discovered alias variable; its model list
then contains only that alias's concrete model. The backend's request options
must be compatible with that family. The @samp{Claude-thinking} configuration uses
this to follow Sonnet with adaptive thinking. A discovery failure makes managed
backends unavailable@comma{} including when a buffer still holds an earlier model. After
a failure@comma{} for example when 1Password could not deliver the API key in time at
startup@comma{} discovery retries after 5 minutes@comma{} 15 minutes and then every hour until
it succeeds. Repeated failures with the same message are warned about once.

@findex gptel-extras-make-claude-provider
Consumers of the @samp{llm} package can call @code{gptel-extras-make-claude-provider} with
an alias variable's symbol. It returns an @samp{llm-claude} provider using the alias's
current concrete model and the @samp{Claude} backend's API key. It rejects unknown
or unresolved aliases. Call it for each request: retaining a provider object
would retain its old model. Magit's commit-message generator accepts such a
provider factory@comma{} so new commit-message requests follow the discovered Haiku.

Discovered models are registered with the backend before defaults change@comma{} so
gptel does not substitute a different model. Existing pricing and request
parameters are preserved for known model IDs. New models use capabilities and
context limits supplied by the API; missing prices remain unknown rather than
being copied from an older release. Automatic discovery cannot guarantee that
future model generations will remain compatible with all request options.

@findex gptel-extras-refresh-models
@vindex gptel-extras-model-discovery-status
@vindex gptel-extras-model-discovery-time
Use @code{gptel-extras-refresh-models} to refresh immediately. Concurrent refreshes
are coalesced. Inspect @code{gptel-extras-model-discovery-status} for @code{refreshing}@comma{}
@code{ready}@comma{} or an error message@comma{} and @code{gptel-extras-model-discovery-time} for the
last successful refresh. A failed refresh displays a warning@comma{} clears the alias
variables@comma{} and marks managed settings unavailable. Requests using those
settings fail explicitly until discovery succeeds; they do not silently use a
stale model or gptel's first registered model. Disabling the mode cancels its
timer and any discovery request; it does not restore previous setting values.

@node Tools
@chapter Tools

@samp{gptel-extras.el} registers several tools that @samp{gptel} can invoke via its tool-use feature. These tools allow the LLM to interact with the filesystem and bibliography.

@menu
* Filesystem tools::
* Bib@TeX{} tools::
* MCP servers::
* Robustifying tool presets::
* Redacting credentials from queries::
@end menu

@node Filesystem tools
@section Filesystem tools

Three filesystem tools are provided@comma{} forming a fallback chain for file modification:

@findex gptel-extras-edit-file
@enumerate
@item
@strong{edit@math{_file}} (@code{gptel-extras-edit-file}): The primary file editing tool. It applies a list of edits to a file@comma{} where each edit specifies an @code{:old_string} to find and a @code{:new_string} to replace it with. The tool first attempts an exact match; if that fails@comma{} it uses fuzzy matching with normalized whitespace@comma{} case-insensitive search@comma{} and flexible regex strategies.

@item
@strong{apply@math{_diff}}: Applies a unified diff (patch) to a file using the external @samp{patch} command. Useful when the LLM generates changes in diff format. Supports configurable patch options (@samp{-p0}@comma{} @samp{-p1}@comma{} etc.).

@item
@strong{replace@math{_file}@math{_contents}}: Replaces the entire content of a file. This is the last resort@comma{} as it requires sending the full file content and is therefore token-inefficient.
@end enumerate

All three tools modify files directly on disk without user confirmation.

@node Bib@TeX{} tools
@section Bib@TeX{} tools

@findex gptel-extras-citar-search
The tool @strong{search@math{_bibliography}} (@code{gptel-extras-citar-search}) searches the user's bibliography via @samp{citar}. It splits the search string into space-separated terms and returns entries matching all terms as whole words. The search is case-insensitive and supports pagination via @samp{limit} and @samp{offset} parameters.

Each result is a pair of @samp{(FORMATTED-CITATION . CITE-KEY)}. The default limit is 10 results; use -1 for no limit.

@findex gptel-extras-add-bib-entry
The tool @strong{add@math{_bib}@math{_entry}} (@code{gptel-extras-add-bib-entry}) adds a bibliographic entry for a URL@comma{} ISBN@comma{} or DOI to a specified Bib@TeX{} file. It delegates to @code{zotra-extras-add-entry} from @samp{zotra-extras.el} and returns the citation key of the newly added entry. Import runs in a neutral buffer@comma{} so an active EWW or Elfeed buffer cannot copy its URL or alter the kill ring.

Both bibliography tools use the importer's direct return value@comma{} so a failed cleanup cannot leave them reporting a previous entry's cached key. Zotra Extras resolves generated-key collisions through Ebib's uniqueness routine before saving and preserves existing records.

@findex gptel-extras-add-bib-entry-and-process
The tool @strong{add@math{_bib}@math{_entry}@math{_and}@math{_process}} (@code{gptel-extras-add-bib-entry-and-process}) imports the entry through Zotra@comma{} loads or refreshes its explicit database in the background@comma{} and calls @code{ebib-extras-process-entry} for key validation@comma{} language handling@comma{} crossref checks@comma{} and attachment lookup. It does not select an Ebib entry@comma{} sort the UI@comma{} or change the caller's buffer or windows. Pass the target bibliography explicitly. The helper refuses an import or refresh when that file has an unsaved visiting buffer or Ebib database. Because Ebib reads a bibliography through the buffer visiting it@comma{} a refresh first reverts an unmodified visiting buffer whose file changed on disk@comma{} so the reload sees the current file rather than the buffer's stale text.

The helper keeps an already valid key and uses the entry's identifier and verified language. Missing language@comma{} ambiguous archive results@comma{} file collisions and unresolved crossrefs produce a blocked result for review. No global input function is replaced; unrelated user commands retain normal input behavior.

The operation retains its original key@comma{} database and entry identity across callbacks. Navigation cannot redirect it. Closing the database@comma{} replacing the entry@comma{} unsaved user edits or an external file change blocks further writes. Existing abstracts are preserved@comma{} including ones added while a request is pending.

To include a staged PDF@comma{} create an operation with @code{(ebib-extras-make-operation KEY DB t)}@comma{} supply it as the fifth argument of @code{ebib-extras-attach-file}@comma{} and then as the fourth argument of @code{gptel-extras--process-bib-entry-headless}. Store and save the verified language before starting. Do not reload the database between these calls.

The result contains @samp{:operation-id}@comma{} the final key@comma{} bibliography filename@comma{} attachment paths@comma{} @samp{:status} (@code{complete}@comma{} @code{pending} or @code{blocked})@comma{} @samp{:pending} task count and @samp{:errors}. Query a timed-out operation with @code{ebib-extras-operation-status-for-id}. Waiting is bounded by @samp{timeout} (45 seconds by default); timeout does not cancel its tasks. An existing PDF does not mark pending abstract@comma{} rendering or OCR work complete. After a blocked operation drains@comma{} resolve its cause and create a new operation for the same existing entry; do not import it again. Verify document identity separately from processing status.

@node MCP servers
@section MCP servers

MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers are meant to start when first used@comma{} not when the configuration loads: @code{gptel-mcp-connect} (in the @samp{gptel-tools} menu) starts the ones whose tools it adds@comma{} and @code{gptel-extras-email-to-calendar} starts the @samp{google-calendar} server if it is not running.

@findex gptel-extras-mcp-secret
The function @code{gptel-extras-mcp-secret} returns a function that reads a credential from a 1Password automation vault with @code{auth-source-extras-op-get}. Use it for each credential in a server's @samp{:env} in @code{mcp-hub-servers}@comma{} so that the credential is read when the server starts rather than when the configuration loads. When the credential cannot be read@comma{} the function signals an error and that server does not start@comma{} instead of starting without it.

@findex gptel-extras-mcp-resolve-env
The function @code{gptel-extras-mcp-resolve-env} is meant as @samp{:filter-args} advice on @code{mcp-connect-server}. @samp{mcp} formats every @samp{:env} value as a string@comma{} so this advice calls each value that is a function and passes on what it returns.

@node Robustifying tool presets
@section Robustifying tool presets

@findex gptel-extras--modify-value-append-fix
The function @code{gptel-extras--modify-value-append-fix} is added as @samp{:around} advice on @code{gptel--modify-value}. It normalizes @samp{:append} specifications in preset plists so that tool names (strings or symbols) are properly wrapped in a list when appending to an existing list value. Without this fix@comma{} appending tool names could be misinterpreted as string appends@comma{} causing errors.

@node Redacting credentials from queries
@section Redacting credentials from queries

@findex gptel-extras-redact-secrets-in-query
@vindex gptel-extras-redact-secrets
The function @code{gptel-extras-redact-secrets-in-query} runs from @code{gptel-prompt-transform-functions} and replaces anything matching @code{simple-extras-secret-regexp} with @samp{[REDACTED-CREDENTIAL]} before the query leaves the machine. It reports how many credentials it removed.

That hook is called in a temporary buffer holding the exact text bound for the provider@comma{} which makes it the one place worth guarding: whatever reaches that buffer is transmitted verbatim to a third party. Guarding individual commands would not be enough@comma{} because a credential can enter a query by many routes — context added from a buffer or a file@comma{} an agent transcript@comma{} a shell history@comma{} or a backtrace whose frames include an API key as an argument. Emacs prints every argument when it enters the debugger@comma{} so a function that takes a key as a parameter puts that key in @samp{*Backtrace*} without anyone typing it.

The function is appended to the hook rather than prepended@comma{} so that it runs @strong{after} @code{gptel--transform-add-context}. Order matters here: context pulled in from other buffers and files is inserted by that earlier function@comma{} and redacting before it ran would leave exactly the material most likely to contain a credential untouched.

The user option @code{gptel-extras-redact-secrets} defaults to @samp{t}; set it to @samp{nil} only to debug the redaction itself.

